        <item>
            <title>Packaging Go dependencies for Gentoo</title>
            <link>https://blog.ferki.it/2024/07/25/packaging-go-dependencies-for-gentoo/</link>
            <guid>https://blog.ferki.it/2024/07/25/packaging-go-dependencies-for-gentoo/</guid>
            <description><![CDATA[
                <p>While I don’t write Go code, I do package Go projects for Gentoo Linux. My
previous post about how to <a href="https://blog.ferki.it/2023/04/24/host-gentoo-dependency-tarballs-as-github-releases/">Host Gentoo dependency tarballs as GitHub
releases</a> got
me invited to give a talk at the <a href="https://www.meetup.com/golang-users-berlin/events/300809374/">Google Developer Group Berlin Golang
meetup</a>, to host
a community round table discussion at GopherCon Europe 2024, and to hold an
online workshop for <a href="https://gentoo-ev.org/">Gentoo e.V.</a></p>

<p>My presentation expands on the above post, and I decided to share the extra
parts in this follow-up, including an idea for Go project maintainers who wish
to streamline the distribution of their work.</p>

                    <p><a href="https://blog.ferki.it/2024/07/25/packaging-go-dependencies-for-gentoo/#section-2">Continue reading...</a></p>

                    <p>Tags:
                        <a href="https://blog.ferki.it/tag/golang/">golang</a>
                        <a href="https://blog.ferki.it/tag/gentoo/">gentoo</a>
                    </p>

            ]]></description>
            <pubDate>
                Thu, 25 Jul 2024 00:00:00 +0000
            </pubDate>
        </item>
